    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 092330\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n430 PM MST Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n.UPDATE...00Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures will prevail through the rest of the \n  week, resulting in widespread moderate Heat Risk and areas of \n  Major Heat Risk.\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings will remain in effect for portions of the\n  lower deserts through the end of the week.\n\n- Thunderstorms chances will continue to focus over Southeastern \n  Arizona and the Eastern Arizona high terrain, with low chances \n  for lower desert storms beginning Thursday and slightly better \n  Friday.\n\n- A pattern shift by the weekend will result in decreasing \n  temperatures and eventually drying conditions from west to east \n  across the forecast area. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nSoutheasterly flow aloft continues over the region as the \nsubtropical high is now centered over Arizona. The high will peak\ntoday with heights ranging between 594-597 dam, continuing to be \nnear to at the max of NAEFS climatological percentile. H5 heights \nwill continue to be unseasonably high, however the positioning of \nthe high will continue to retrograde further to the southwest, and\nweaken/flatten slightly by the end of the week. Despite the\npositioning of the high, afternoon temperatures will continue to \nbe 3-7 degrees above normal for mid September. Morning low \ntemperatures are also being affected and are expected to remain \n8-10 degrees above normal through at least the end of the week,\nproviding very little relief from the heat during the day. A gulf\nsurge last night brought an increase in moisture into the lower \ndeserts. This can especially be seen on this morning's 12Z \nsounding where compared to 24 hours ago PWATs increased from 1.47\"\nto 1.79\" and mean mixing ratios increased from 8.5 g/kg to 13.6 \ng/kg. This increase is also notable in the morning dew points, \nwhere SC AZ had dew points in the mid to upper 60 and the western\nlower deserts were even higher in the mid to upper 70s. This \nmoisture and thick overnight cloud coverage, kept lows well above\nnormal, even setting new record warm lows by 3F-6F at all three \nof the climate cites (Phoenix, Yuma, and El Centro). With the \nabove normal high and low temperatures, in conjunction with more \nhumid conditions, widespread Moderate to Major HeatRisk remain \nover the region, especially over the lower desert areas. Due to \nthis the Extreme Heat Warning remains in place for portions of the\nlower deserts through the end of the week. Since the region has \nmore moisture in place/ higher dew points the heat will be less \nmanageable and individuals should take extra care when spending \ntime outdoors, as apparent temperatures will be closer to \n108F-117F, with the high range most common across SW AZ and SE CA.\n\nWhile heat is currently a dominating talking point, thunderstorm \nchances are also worth mentioning. As mentioned above, we have \nplenty of moisture in place over the region, and GFS forecast \nsoundings show MUCAPE values between 500-750 J/kg late tonight \ninto the overnight period. During that window though CIN will \nalso be quite high (between -100 to -150 J/kg) so development in \nthe lower deserts remains unfavorable. The latest CAMs are still \nshowing good signal for development in southeastern AZ, and are \ntrying to show isolated showers developing in Pinal County late \ntonight, although like the past few nights this will remain \nconditional. In addition, the HREF shows between a 10-30% chance \nof thunderstorms mostly confined to the far eastern high terrain,\nwhile the best chances remain in southeastern AZ outside of our \nCWA, both tonight and again Thursday night.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...    \nThe upper level pattern will begin to shift Friday, as troughing \nenters the Northwest US and displaces the subtropical high east of\nthe region this weekend. Flow aloft will transition back out of \nthe southwest, ending the brief, more typical monsoon flow pattern\nexperienced during the middle of the work week to something more \nakin to a transition season setup. However, before more pronounced\nsouthwesterly drying begins in earnest this weekend, weaker flow \naloft and perhaps the best moisture profiles of the week over \nSouth- Central AZ (near-surface mixing ratios 11-12 g/kg) will \npresent an opportunity for at least one active monsoon \nthunderstorm day across the lower deserts (if the highly \nconditional threat is not realized on Thursday). KPHX GFS bufr \nsoundings, which admittedly have been very sensitive to subtle \nchanges in low level moisture, indicate a balance of CAPE/DCAPE \naround 1500 J/kg late Friday afternoon into the early evening, \nwith weak (greater than -50 J/kg) CIN for several hours. On \nSaturday, thermodynamics also look similarly favorable, with the \ncap almost completely disappearing. Both days, the threat is once\nagain highly conditional, as there is no obvious upper level \nascent mechanism.\n\nEnsembles continue to disagree on how far south the jet will dip \nwith the incoming Northwest US troughing this weekend, however, it\nwill likely remain far enough removed that jet dynamics do not \ncome into play to influence thunderstorm chances locally. \nRegardless, moisture will not be entirely scoured out of \nEastern/Southeastern AZ, and daily monsoon thunderstorm activity \nwill be maintained over prominent terrain features of Eastern AZ,\nSoutheastern AZ, and Sonora into next week. Eventually, if the \nsubtropical high can reassert itself east of the region and/or \ntroughing weakens and retreats north, daily convection to our \nsouth could push better moisture into South-Central AZ and once \nagain give our area a shot at more widespread thunderstorm chances\nby the middle of next week. Anticipate temperatures to decrease \nback into a near normal range over the weekend as a result of the \npattern shift, and possibly slightly below normal next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2330Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns will exist through the forecast\nperiod under occasional passing mid to high lvl clouds. Westerly\nwinds will continue through sunset with intermittent gusts into\nthe upper teens possible. There is a moderate potential for a weak\noutflow boundary to reach KIWA and KPHX tonight, causing an \nearlier than usual easterly shift between 05Z-07Z. Any lingering \nshower activity should remain well south of the Phoenix Metro this\nevening and overnight.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather concerns will exist through the TAF period under\nperiods of mid/high cigs. Winds should follow typical diurnal\npatterns at both terminals with speeds remaining aob 8kts along\nwith extended periods of nearly calm conditions during the \nevening and overnight hours. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAbove normal temperatures will prevail through the end of the work \nweek, then temperatures are likely to decrease closer to normal over \nthe weekend. Afternoon minimum humidities will generally range \nbetween 18-30% areawide through much of the week following fair to \ngood overnight recoveries between 35-70%. Fairly typical diurnal \nwinds with occasional afternoon upslope breeziness can be expected \nthrough Thursday, followed by an increase in southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds Friday into this weekend. Rain and thunderstorm \nchances will remain low and mostly limited to the eastern Arizona \nhigh terrain through midweek before slight chances become introduced \nto the lower deserts of at least the eastern districts late week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530-532-533.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Thursday for AZZ531-535>544-\n     546-548>551-559.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-566-567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Whittock \nAVIATION...Salerno\nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock\n",
